\title{A Sharp $\sqrt{2}$ Threshold for the Vietoris--Rips Complex of the Standard Simplex}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 14, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study the Vietoris--Rips complex of the canonical high-symmetry configuration
in Euclidean space: the $n$ standard basis vectors $e_1, \dots, e_n$ of $\R^n$,
which are pairwise separated by the exact distance $\sqrt{2}$. We prove that this
configuration exhibits a \emph{perfectly sharp} threshold at scale $\sqrt{2}$. At
every scale $r$ with $0 \le r < \sqrt{2}$ the complex collapses to its vertices,
containing exactly $n+1$ simplices; at scale $r = \sqrt{2}$ it becomes the full
power set, containing exactly $2^n$ simplices. For $n \ge 2$ this is a strict jump
from a linear count to an exponential count, concentrated at a single scale, with
no intermediate structure. We localise the exponential blow-up under multiplicative
interleavings: for any one-sided $c$-approximation $G$, at least $2^n$ simplices
must be stored by scale $c\sqrt{2}$, while at most $n+1$ simplices can be present at
any scale $t$ with $ct < \sqrt{2}$. We show $2^n$ is globally extremal --- no
proximity graph on $n$ vertices produces more than $2^n$ cliques --- so the
standard simplex attains maximal complexity at $\sqrt{2}$. Finally, we contrast
this all-or-nothing jump with a \emph{graded} construction achieving a positive
sub-threshold exponential rate $\gamma(c) = (\sqrt{2}/c - 1)/(\sqrt{2}-1)$,
isolating a dichotomy: sharpness of the threshold and sub-threshold exponential
hardness are mutually exclusive, and the latter requires graded geometry.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

The Vietoris--Rips complex is a fundamental construction linking metric geometry to
combinatorial topology and to the practice of topological data analysis (TDA).
Given a finite metric space $(X, d)$ and a scale $r \ge 0$, it records which finite
subsets of $X$ have all pairwise distances at most $r$. As $r$ increases these
complexes grow monotonically, forming a \emph{filtration} whose persistent homology
summarises the multiscale shape of $X$.

The typical narrative around such filtrations is one of \emph{gradual}
accumulation. In this paper we isolate the opposite, extremal behaviour. We
consider the most symmetric configuration available in dimension $n$ --- the
standard orthonormal basis of $\R^n$ --- and show that its Vietoris--Rips
filtration does \emph{nothing} until scale $\sqrt{2}$ and then attains \emph{maximal}
complexity instantaneously. The threshold is perfectly sharp: there is no scale at
which the complex is partially built.

Our contributions are: (1) a complete determination of the simplex count exhibiting
a strict $n+1 \to 2^n$ jump at $\sqrt{2}$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}); (2) two-sided
interleaving bounds localising the blow-up to the $\sqrt{2}$ scale
(Theorems~\ref{thm:lower}--\ref{thm:upper}); (3) global extremality of the $2^n$
count (Theorem~\ref{thm:extremal}); and (4) a structural dichotomy between sharp
and graded geometries (Section~\ref{sec:dichotomy}).

\section{Preliminaries and definitions}

Throughout, $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and we work in $\R^n$ with its standard $\ell^2$
metric $\dist(x,y) = \sqrt{\sum_{k=1}^n (x_k - y_k)^2}$.

\begin{definition}[Canonical configuration]
For $1 \le i \le n$, let $e_i \in \R^n$ denote the $i$-th standard basis vector.
The \emph{canonical configuration} is the point set $\{e_1, \dots, e_n\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Vietoris--Rips simplex]
For a scale $r \in \R$, a subset $S \subseteq \{1,\dots,n\}$ is a
\emph{Vietoris--Rips simplex at scale $r$} if $\dist(e_i, e_j) \le r$ for all
$i, j \in S$. The condition is vacuous for $|S| \le 1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Vietoris--Rips complex]
The \emph{Vietoris--Rips complex at scale $r$}, written $\VR_n(r)$, is the
collection of all Vietoris--Rips simplices at scale $r$. We write $|\VR_n(r)|$ for
its number of simplices.
\end{definition}

\section{The exact $\sqrt{2}$ geometry}

\begin{lemma}[Exact $\sqrt{2}$ distance]\label{lem:dist}
For $i \neq j$, $\dist(e_i, e_j) = \sqrt{2}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The difference $e_i - e_j$ has $i$-th coordinate $1$, $j$-th coordinate $-1$, and
all others $0$. Hence $\dist(e_i,e_j)^2 = 1^2 + (-1)^2 = 2$, and the nonnegative
square root is $\sqrt{2}$.
\end{proof}

Since all nonzero pairwise distances equal $\sqrt{2}$, the configuration is
\emph{equidistant}: a regular simplex realised by orthonormal vectors. This
degeneracy of the distance spectrum produces the sharp threshold.

\section{The sharp threshold}

\begin{proposition}[At the threshold: full power set]\label{prop:at}
At scale $\sqrt{2}$ every subset is a simplex, so
$\VR_n(\sqrt{2}) = 2^{\{1,\dots,n\}}$ and $|\VR_n(\sqrt{2})| = 2^n$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
For any $S$ and $i, j \in S$: if $i = j$ then $\dist(e_i,e_j) = 0 \le \sqrt2$; if
$i \neq j$ then $\dist(e_i,e_j) = \sqrt2 \le \sqrt2$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:dist}. Thus
every subset is a simplex, and the power set of an $n$-element set has $2^n$
elements.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Below the threshold: collapse]\label{prop:below}
Let $0 \le r < \sqrt{2}$. Then $S$ is a simplex at scale $r$ if and only if
$|S| \le 1$; consequently $|\VR_n(r)| = n+1$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
If $|S| \le 1$ the condition is vacuous. Conversely, if $S$ is a simplex with
$|S| \ge 2$, pick distinct $a, b \in S$; then $\dist(e_a,e_b) \le r$, contradicting
$\dist(e_a,e_b) = \sqrt2 > r$ from Lemma~\ref{lem:dist}. The subsets of size at most
one are the empty set and the $n$ singletons, totalling $n+1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp $\sqrt{2}$ threshold]\label{thm:sharp}
For the canonical configuration in $\R^n$,
\[
|\VR_n(r)| =
\begin{cases}
n+1, & 0 \le r < \sqrt{2},\\
2^n, & r = \sqrt{2}.
\end{cases}
\]
For $n \ge 2$ the transition is a strict increase $n+1 < 2^n$ concentrated at the
single scale $\sqrt{2}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The two cases are Propositions~\ref{prop:below} and \ref{prop:at}. For $n \ge 2$,
induct: $n=2$ gives $3 < 4$; and $m+1 < 2^m$ implies
$m+2 < 2^m + 2^m = 2^{m+1}$.
\end{proof}

The threshold is sharp in the literal sense: for every $\varepsilon > 0$ the complex
at scale $\sqrt{2} - \varepsilon$ still has only $n+1$ simplices.

\section{Interleaving: localising the blow-up}

\begin{definition}[$c$-approximation]
Let $c \ge 1$. A family $G : \R \to \{\text{finite complexes on } n \text{ vertices}\}$
is a \emph{one-sided $c$-approximation} of the canonical filtration if:
(i) $c \ge 1$;
(ii) \emph{(completeness)} $\VR_n(t) \subseteq G(c t)$ for all $t \ge 0$;
(iii) \emph{(soundness)} $G(t) \subseteq \VR_n(c t)$ for all $t \ge 0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Exponential survival at the threshold]\label{thm:lower}
For any $c$-approximation $G$, $\ 2^n \le |G(c\sqrt{2})|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By completeness at $t = \sqrt2$, $\VR_n(\sqrt2) \subseteq G(c\sqrt2)$, so
$|G(c\sqrt2)| \ge |\VR_n(\sqrt2)| = 2^n$ by Proposition~\ref{prop:at}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Polynomial ceiling below the threshold]\label{thm:upper}
For any $c$-approximation $G$ and any $t \ge 0$ with $ct < \sqrt2$,
$\ |G(t)| \le n+1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By soundness, $G(t) \subseteq \VR_n(ct)$. Since $ct \ge 0$ and $ct < \sqrt2$,
Proposition~\ref{prop:below} gives $|\VR_n(ct)| = n+1$, whence
$|G(t)| \le n+1$.
\end{proof}

Together, Theorems~\ref{thm:lower} and \ref{thm:upper} sandwich the transition: the
entire exponential cost is localised to the $\sqrt{2}$ scale and cannot be smeared
out by any bounded multiplicative approximation.

\section{Global extremality}

\begin{theorem}[Extremality]\label{thm:extremal}
Let $H$ be any simple graph on $\{1,\dots,n\}$, and let its clique complex be the
family of all vertex subsets inducing a complete subgraph. Then the number of
cliques of $H$ is at most $2^n$, so
\[
(\text{number of cliques of } H) \le |\VR_n(\sqrt{2})| = 2^n,
\]
and the canonical configuration attains the maximum on $n$ points.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Every clique is a subset of the $n$-element vertex set, of which there are $2^n$;
hence the clique complex has at most $2^n$ elements, with equality for the complete
graph. Proposition~\ref{prop:at} identifies $\VR_n(\sqrt2)$ with the full power set.
\end{proof}

\section{The structural dichotomy: sharp versus graded}\label{sec:dichotomy}

The sharpness of Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp} comes at a cost. To prove
\emph{sub-threshold} hardness --- that even a coarse approximation valid strictly
below $\sqrt{2}$ must be exponentially large --- the canonical configuration is
powerless: by Theorem~\ref{thm:upper} it offers only $n+1$ simplices below the
interleaved threshold. The all-or-nothing jump concentrates all complexity at one
scale.

Sub-threshold exponential lower bounds require a \emph{graded geometry}, in which
the pairwise distances fill the window $(1, \sqrt{2}]$ so that structure accumulates
progressively. One builds a finite metric on $n$ points whose active cliques have
radius $\rho(n,i) = 1 + (\sqrt{2}-1)(i+1)/n$, and defines the \emph{effective rate}
\[
\gamma(c) = \frac{\sqrt{2}/c - 1}{\sqrt{2} - 1}, \qquad c \in [1, \sqrt{2}),
\]
which satisfies $0 < \gamma(c) \le 1$ and $\lim_{c \to \sqrt{2}^-}\gamma(c) = 0$.
The graded construction then yields, for any one-sided $c$-approximation $G$,
\[
2^{\lfloor n\,\gamma(c)\rfloor} \le |G(\sqrt{2})|,
\]
an exponential sub-threshold bound whose rate is positive for every $c < \sqrt2$ and
degrades continuously to zero at the $\sqrt2$ barrier.

The dichotomy: the equidistant standard basis is perfectly sharp but has
$\gamma \equiv 0$; a graded spectrum is gradual but has $\gamma(c) > 0$. One can have
a sharp threshold or sub-threshold exponential hardness, but not both from the same
geometry.

\section{Applications and significance}

Theorems~\ref{thm:lower}--\ref{thm:upper} give a concrete exact instance where
multiplicative interleaving cannot reduce the worst-case size of a Vietoris--Rips
representation: the exponential cost is intrinsic and merely shifts by the factor
$c$ in scale. The standard simplex is a clean benchmark for claims about the shape
of a filtration near a critical scale, and the dichotomy of
Section~\ref{sec:dichotomy} is a design principle: exponential lower bounds
\emph{approaching} a critical scale require a graded distance spectrum, with
$\gamma(c)$ quantifying how much sub-threshold hardness grading buys.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Geometry-to-rate dictionary]
The guaranteed sub-threshold exponential rate $\gamma(c)$ of a finite point cloud is
governed by how quickly its sorted pairwise-distance spectrum accumulates just below
$\sqrt{2}$: a flat spectrum forces $\gamma \equiv 0$, a uniformly graded spectrum
forces the maximal $\gamma$; the rate is a functional of the distribution of
near-threshold distances.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Euclidean realisability of the graded rate]
The graded rate $\gamma(c) = (\sqrt{2}/c-1)/(\sqrt{2}-1)$ is realisable by an
explicit point cloud in $\R^d$ (e.g. concentric scaled regular simplices with radii
sweeping $(1,\sqrt{2}]$), so sub-$\sqrt{2}$ exponential lower bounds survive in
genuine Euclidean space.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Sharpness equals spectral degeneracy]
A finite configuration has a strictly sharp Vietoris--Rips threshold at scale $\rho$
if and only if all its nonzero pairwise distances equal $\rho$, i.e. it is
equidistant up to scaling.
\end{conjecture}

Further directions include quantitative stability of the collapse under metric
perturbation, and upgrading the counting bounds to lower bounds on interleaved
persistence modules and barcodes.

\section{Conclusion}

The standard basis of $\R^n$ has a Vietoris--Rips filtration that is inert with
$n+1$ simplices below $\sqrt{2}$ and maximal with $2^n$ simplices at $\sqrt{2}$, a
strict exponential jump at a single scale. This blow-up is intrinsic: approximations
shift but cannot avoid it, and $2^n$ is the global maximum on $n$ points. The
degeneracy of the distance spectrum makes the threshold sharp and, for the same
reason, silent below $\sqrt{2}$ --- a limitation overcome by graded geometry with
rate $\gamma(c)$. The result is a compact meeting point of Euclidean geometry,
enumerative combinatorics, and the approximation theory of persistent topology.

